# The engine commit this client's fused build is known to build against. The
# client fuses with the engine as a mod (mmo/mods/openmmo), so it is authored
# against engine sources that move on their own. `make -C mmo enginecheck`
# compares the engine checkout's HEAD with the commit below and warns on drift;
# `make -C mmo fused` runs that check first, so a build that breaks after an
# engine bump says "the engine moved" instead of failing deep in engine code.
#
# The sha is half the contract. The other half is mmo/ENGINE_SURFACE: the engine
# paths this build depends on by content, each with the hash it had when this
# commit was verified. A sha alone cannot say whether an engine that moved moved
# anywhere that matters.
#
# To bump, in this order: `make -C mmo pincheck` and read every diff it names;
# `make -C mmo patchcheck`; `make -C mmo fused` plus the five fused checks in
# `make test`; `make -C mmo abicheck`. Then replace the sha, run
# `mmo/tools/engine_surface.sh --update`, and commit the two together. pincheck
# fails on a sha whose manifest was written for a different one, so half a bump
# cannot pass unnoticed.
#
# This is the engine/pokeplatinum submodule's own commit, so a fresh clone with
# `--recurse-submodules` is already at it.
